From: Codex <codex@openai.com>
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] wmediumd: reject stale receive-frequency clones

The frequency filter deliberately treated a radio with no transmit-learned
VIF as eligible for multicast on every frequency.  After each wmediumd
restart, every 2.4 GHz beacon was consequently cloned toward idle 5 GHz WLAN
clients.  Linux rejected those HWSIM_CMD_FRAME messages with EINVAL.  Sequence
correlation of the errors identified only multicast beacons sent to radios
whose active frequency had not yet been learned.

An hwsim radio which is scanning or joining transmits a probe/authentication
frame before it needs the matching response.  That frame already updates the
VIF owner and frequency map.  Do not guess a receive frequency before this
evidence exists.  This removes the rejected clone flood while retaining
multicast on every transmit-learned channel context of a multi-channel radio.

The same evidence must be checked at delivery time for unicast.  A client can
transmit a 2.4 GHz probe request, advance its scan to 5 GHz, and leave directed
probe responses queued on the old channel.  Recheck the receiver immediately
before cloning a unicast frame so stale scan responses are discarded in
userspace instead of rejected by the kernel.

---
 wmediumd/wmediumd.c | 18 +++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/wmediumd/wmediumd.c b/wmediumd/wmediumd.c
--- a/wmediumd/wmediumd.c
+++ b/wmediumd/wmediumd.c
@@ -428,22 +428,20 @@
 /* A BPI radio can host VIFs on several channel contexts, while a client radio
  * normally has only one.  Broadcast/multicast delivery must therefore match
  * any frequency learned for the receiving radio, not blindly clone every
- * beacon to every hwsim radio.  Keep unknown radios eligible during bootstrap
- * until their first transmitted frame reveals an active channel. */
+ * beacon to every hwsim radio.  Unknown radios first transmit while scanning;
+ * that frame teaches their active frequency before a response is delivered. */
 static int mc_station_receives_freq(struct station *station, u32 freq)
 {
 	size_t i;
-	int has_known_freq = 0;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < mc_n_vifs; i++) {
 		if (!mc_vifs[i].freq_known || mc_vifs[i].owner != station)
 			continue;
-		has_known_freq = 1;
 		if (mc_vifs[i].freq == freq)
 			return 1;
 	}
 
-	return !has_known_freq;
+	return 0;
 }
 
 enum ack_eligibility { ACK_ELIGIBLE, ACK_OFFCHANNEL, ACK_FREQ_UNKNOWN };
@@ -1020,6 +1018,12 @@
 						      frame->cookie);
 
 			} else if (station_has_addr(station, dest)) {
+				/* A scan may move the receiver after this response was
+				 * queued.  Recheck at delivery time instead of asking the
+				 * kernel to reject a stale off-channel clone. */
+				if (!mc_station_receives_freq(station, frame->freq))
+					continue;
+
 				if (set_interference_duration(ctx,
 					frame->sender->index, frame->freq,
 					frame->duration, frame->signal))
@@ -1649,8 +1653,8 @@
 			printf("PASS delivery: learned VIF resolves to its owning radio without ADD_MAC_ADDR replay\n");
 		else { printf("FAIL delivery: learned VIF did not resolve to owning radio\n"); fails++; }
 		if (mc_station_receives_freq(&r3, 5180) &&
 		    !mc_station_receives_freq(&r3, 2437) &&
-		    mc_station_receives_freq(&apa, 2437))
-			printf("PASS multicast: known radios reject off-channel clones; unknown radios bootstrap\n");
+		    !mc_station_receives_freq(&apa, 2437))
+			printf("PASS multicast: known radios match frequency; unknown radios wait for TX learning\n");
 		else { printf("FAIL multicast: receiver frequency eligibility\n"); fails++; }
 
